{
  "id": "w1-09-remember",
  "world": 1,
  "number": 9,
  "title": "Remember",
  "format": "build",
  "skills": [
    "logic.latch",
    "logic.feedback",
    "chip.pinout"
  ],
  "brief": {
    "story": "The club's 3D printer needs a start/stop control: press START and it runs until someone presses STOP, like every machine in a workshop. A button only lights the LED while it's held. The circuit has to remember. IC1 (74HC00) has START on pin 1 and STOP on pin 5, each held high by a pull-up and pulled low by its button, and the RUN LED on pin 3.",
    "goal": "Cross-couple two NAND gates into a latch: START turns RUN on and it stays on after you let go; STOP turns it off and it stays off.",
    "datasheet": {
      "title": "SR latch from two NANDs",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Gate 1",
          "pins 1 (START), 2 → 3 (RUN)"
        ],
        [
          "Gate 2",
          "pins 4, 5 (STOP) → 6"
        ],
        [
          "Feedback",
          "3 → 4 and 6 → 2"
        ],
        [
          "Buttons",
          "pull to 0 when pressed"
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
  "hints": [
    "A latch is two gates feeding each other: each one's output goes into one of the other's inputs. That loop is the memory.",
    "Gate 1's output is pin 3 (column 12); gate 2's spare input is pin 4 (column 13). Gate 2's output is pin 6 (column 15); gate 1's spare input is pin 2 (column 11).",
    "Two wires: column 12 to column 13, and column 15 to column 11 (rows a–d)."
  ],
